<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Caucasus</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Armenia (Artaxiad)</h1><p class="st-meta">189 BCE – 12 CE (approx.) · capital Artaxata</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Artaxiad dynasty turned Armenia into a major power, and under Tigranes the Great it briefly ruled an empire stretching from the Caspian to the Mediterranean.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artaxias-i">Artaxias</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid</a> governor, broke away after the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a> lost the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-magnesia">Battle of Magnesia</a> in 190 BCE and proclaimed himself king of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-armenia-arsacid">Greater Armenia</a>. He united the Armenian-speaking lands, set boundary stones that still survive, and founded a new capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artaxata">Artaxata</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aras-river">Araxes</a>, according to legend on the advice of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The kingdom combined an Iranian-style monarchy and great noble houses with Hellenistic court culture; Greek plays were staged at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artaxata">Artaxata</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigranes-the-great">Tigranes II</a>, the Great, ruling from 95 BCE, seized northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/syrian-arab-republic">Syria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a>, took the title King of Kings and built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigranocerta">Tigranocerta</a>, settling it with deportees. Wealth came from farming, horses and trade routes between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthia">Parthia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucius-licinius-lucullus">Lucullus</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigranes-the-great">Tigranes</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigranocerta">Tigranocerta</a> in 69 BCE, and in 66 BCE he submitted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a>, keeping only <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/armenia">Armenia</a>. His successors were caught between the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/parthian-empire">Parthian Empire</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artavasdes-ii-of-armenia">Artavasdes II</a> was killed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. After rival puppet kings, the line ended about 12 CE.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-armenia-artaxiad.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Armenia (Artaxiad)" width="320" height="312"><figcaption>Image: Classical Numismatic Group; [1] · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coin_of_Tigranes_II_the_Great,_Antioch_mint.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>189 BCE – 12 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Artaxata</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Artaxias I, Tigranes II the Great, Artavasdes II</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Armenian paganism with Iranian and Greek influence</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Armenian; Greek at court</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/armenia">Armenia</a>, <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>, <a href="/countries/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>, <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Artaxiad Armenia, Greater Armenia, Armenian Empire of Tigranes</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-88&amp;focus=kingdom-of-armenia-artaxiad" aria-label="Kingdom of Armenia (Artaxiad) on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5759 795 640 